STM32F769I-DISCO firmware change log
===================================

Unreleased
----------

Added (issue #14)
* FreeRTOS-Kernel V11.3.3 with the GCC Cortex-M7 r0p1 port, a 1 kHz tick,
  stack-overflow and allocation-failure hooks, and assertion diagnostics.
* A statically allocated `platform` task owns console processing, raw lwIP,
  and the touch UI. Board initialization and both POST phases still complete
  before the scheduler starts.
* Console command `rtos` reports scheduler state, tick count, free/minimum
  heap, and the platform task's stack high-water mark.
* Static idle and timer task storage plus heap scheme 4 (64 KiB) for future
  dynamically created RTOS objects.

Changed (issue #14)
* FreeRTOS now owns SVC and PendSV. SysTick advances the HAL tick at all times
  and the FreeRTOS tick after scheduler startup, preserving every existing
  bounded HAL timeout and software network clock.
* Bare-metal lwIP remains deliberately configured with `NO_SYS=1`; all raw
  stack calls and callbacks execute in the single platform task.

Memory impact (issue #14, arm-none-eabi-size, -Og)
* Flash text 123,524 -> 131,344 bytes; DTCM data+bss is 99,104 bytes with
  approximately 31 KiB remaining before ETHRAM. Ethernet DMA placement is
  unchanged.

Fixed (issue #12)
* Moved the zero-copy Ethernet RX pool from DTCM, which Ethernet DMA cannot
  access, into the MPU-protected non-cacheable ETHRAM region. The linker now
  asserts that descriptors and RX buffers remain below the fixed lwIP heap at
  0x20025000.
* Ethernet MAC initialization failures now propagate through ethernetif_init()
  and netif_add(); DHCP is not started for a failed interface. Transmit errors
  are returned to lwIP instead of being reported as successful packets.
* USART console reception and diagnostic output now start before I2C, SDRAM,
  display, touch, and Ethernet initialization. Boot-stage messages identify
  the subsystem reached if later initialization stalls or fails.
* POST is split into an early CPU/clock/USART/CRC phase and a peripheral phase.
  The console `test post` command still runs the complete POST suite.
  Display-specific PLLSAI validation remains in the late LTDC test.
* The stdout hook returns a short write or EIO when HAL_UART_Transmit() fails
  instead of silently claiming every byte was sent.

Memory impact (issue #12, arm-none-eabi-size, -Og)
* Flash text 122,732 -> 123,524 bytes. The RX pool moved from DTCM into the
  19,139-byte DMA section; DTCM data+bss fell from 48,716 to 23,432 bytes. The lwIP heap was
  moved from 0x20020400 to 0x20025000 to follow the DMA-owned pool.

Added (issue #10)
* DNS configuration from DHCP: LWIP_DNS is enabled, so the DHCP client
  installs the option 6 servers (up to two). Changes are logged.
* NTP time synchronization from DHCP: option 42 is requested
  (LWIP_DHCP_GET_NTP_SRV, up to two servers) and handed to a project-owned
  SNTPv4 client (LWIP/App/sntp_client.c, raw UDP API; the local lwIP tree has
  no apps/sntp). Replies are validated (source address/port, mode, version,
  stratum, leap indicator, kiss-o'-death, originate-timestamp cookie), the
  time is compensated by half the round trip, servers fail over after a 3 s
  timeout, and the clock resynchronizes hourly (30 s retry after a full failed
  round). No server is hard-coded; without option 42 the firmware reports that.
* Software UTC clock (Sntp_GetUnixTimeMs()): time of the last reply plus the
  HAL tick, re-based daily so tick arithmetic never wraps.
* Network screen rows DNS 1, DNS 2, NTP server, NTP status, and UTC time; the
  home status line shows UTC time once synchronized.
* Console: "ntp [sync]", "dns <hostname>"; "net" lists DNS and NTP servers.
* On-demand tests "dns" (resolve example.com) and "ntp" (fresh
  synchronization, plausible time and stratum); not part of the POST because
  they need a DHCP lease.

Fixed (issue #10)
* LWIP_RAND() is rand(), which was never seeded, so DHCP transaction IDs and
  the DNS ID/source-port randomization of LWIP_DNS_SECURE repeated on every
  boot. It is now seeded at the first link-up from the device UID and timing
  jitter (not cryptographic).

Memory impact (issue #10, arm-none-eabi-size, -Og)
* Flash text 112,048 -> 122,732 bytes; DTCM data+bss 40,828 -> 48,716 bytes
  (DNS table, SNTP state, larger lwIP pool configuration). The 10 KiB lwIP heap
  stays in the Ethernet RAM region (LWIP_RAM_HEAP_POINTER 0x20020400).

Added (issue #7)
* Touch user interface (ui.c): a home screen with NETWORK and LOG buttons and a
  status line, a Network screen (link, speed/duplex, DHCP state, IP address,
  netmask, gateway, DNS, MAC, uptime), and a Log screen; both detail screens
  have a BACK button. The UI lays out in top-left coordinates and converts to
  the display driver's bottom-left origin; touch Y is converted the same way.
* On-screen printf() log: _write() copies all stdout text into a 64-line ring
  buffer (log_buffer.c, 66 columns), rendered with font_dot_10x14 (12 x 16
  cells, 26 visible rows). Only changed characters are redrawn, at most every
  100 ms.
* Console commands "net", "screen [home|network|log]", and "tap <x> <y>"
  (top-left pixel coordinates) for remote UI and network checks.
* POST test "eth": lwIP started, LAN8742 detected, PHY ID 0x0007:0xC13x; link
  and DHCP state are reported for information.
* Ethernet link changes and DHCP leases are logged through printf().

Changed (issue #7)
* Bare-metal lwIP/Ethernet is enabled again: MX_LWIP_Init() runs after touch
  initialization and MX_LWIP_Process() runs in the main loop. The fixed 2.5 s
  delay and 2 s PHY wait were removed; PHY detection is retried by the 100 ms
  link poll, so boot no longer blocks.
* The crosshair touch demo (display.c, Display_Run()) was replaced by the UI;
  touch_activated_flag is now declared in ft6206.h.
* The intrusive "display" test repaints the current UI screen afterwards.

Fixed (issue #7)
* dhcp_start() was called on an administratively down interface and failed
  ("netif is not up"); the interface is now brought up before DHCP starts.
* The Ethernet MAC address pointer handed to HAL referenced a stack array; it
  is now static.
* A failed HAL_ETH_Init() no longer halts the board in Error_Handler(); it is
  logged and networking stays down.

Memory impact (issue #7, arm-none-eabi-size, -Og)
* Flash text 52,292 -> 112,048 bytes (lwIP linked in again, UI, fonts).
* DTCM data+bss 9,256 -> 40,828 bytes (lwIP memory pools ~30 KiB, log buffer
  4.3 KiB). At that revision the lwIP RX pool was incorrectly placed in DTCM;
  issue #12 relocates it to dedicated Ethernet RAM.

Changed
* Separated application processing from order-sensitive board initialization
  through App_Init() and non-blocking App_Process() entry points.
* Removed obsolete CubeMX USER CODE markers and empty generated section comments
  from project-owned startup, interrupt, MSP, and lwIP integration files.
* Reduced cyclic header dependencies and made module includes explicit.
* Documented the HAL/CMSIS boundary and the steps required before enabling
  FreeRTOS.
* Added contracts and member descriptions for important application, display,
  touch, and SDRAM interfaces.
* Made the interrupt-shared touch activation state volatile and gave the touch
  device a typed I2C handle.
* Decoupled SDRAM access from the DMA2D output-address register, added null,
  alignment, overflow, and full-range validation, and made the busy timeout safe
  across HAL tick wraparound.
* Added a non-destructive power-on self-test (13 tests) and an interactive
  USART1 command console (help, list, test, led, bright, info, reset). Tests
  cover core/MPU, clocks and SysTick, USART1, CRC known answers, I2C1/I2C4
  scans, FT6206 identification, WM8994 chip ID, SDRAM probe, DMA2D fill, LTDC
  refresh rate, DSI PHY and DCS path, and GPIO. Intrusive tests ("sdramfull",
  "display") overwrite only the unused upper 8 MiB of SDRAM or briefly draw
  color bars, and run only on request.
* USART1 now runs in TX/RX mode with interrupt-driven reception (IRQ priority
  6) into a single-producer/single-consumer ring buffer.

Fixed
* printf() hung forever in ITM_SendChar() unless a debugger captured SWO: the
  ITM was enabled but the TPIU was never configured, so the stimulus FIFO never
  drained and the firmware stalled before the POST or the application loop.
  ITM_Init() now configures the TPIU (NRZ, 1.8 MHz) and ITM writes are bounded.
* stdout UART writes use a bounded timeout instead of HAL_MAX_DELAY, and stdout
  is unbuffered.
* A failed App_Init() no longer halts silently in Error_Handler(); the POST runs
  first, the failure is printed, and the console stays available.
* SDRAM bring-up now waits the JEDEC power-up time after clock enable, uses
  bounded command timeouts, and programs the refresh counter through
  HAL_SDRAM_ProgramRefreshRate() instead of OR-ing into FMC_SDRTR.
* SDRAM_CMD_SELFREFRESH_MODE was 4 (load mode register); corrected to 5.
* FT6206: the touch interrupt flag is consumed before the I2C sample, so an
  edge arriving during the transfer is no longer lost; the debounce deadline
  comparison is wrap-safe; the identification read result is checked instead
  of inspecting an uninitialized buffer; TD_STATUS values above 2 (0x0F/0xFF
  when idle) are decoded as no contact instead of three phantom contacts.
* Display_FillRectangle() rejects zero-sized rectangles and bounds the DMA2D
  wait to 100 ms, returning the transfer status.
* GET_POSITIOIN_ADDRESS renamed to GET_POSITION_ADDRESS with fully
  parenthesized arguments.
* Display_Run() ignores a sample whose I2C read failed; touch coordinates are
  formatted with snprintf().
* Redundant DMA2D register writes in MX_DMA2D_Init() were removed (every
  HAL_DMA2D_Start() reprograms them); the LTDC line interrupt is armed through
  HAL_LTDC_ProgramLineEvent().
* Error_Handler() lights LD_USER1 so a fatal stop is visible.

Memory impact (arm-none-eabi-size, -Og)
* Flash text 38,812 -> 52,292 bytes; DTCM data+bss 7,840 -> 9,256 bytes.
* Ethernet RAM, stack/heap reservation and framebuffer layout unchanged. SDRAM
  0xC0800000-0xC0FFFFFF is used only as scratch by the intrusive tests.

Known limitations
* FreeRTOS is not yet included or started; lwIP remains configured for NO_SYS.
* The synchronized time is not written to the hardware RTC, so it is lost on
  reset until the next SNTP exchange. Only UTC is shown (no time zone).
* Failover to the second NTP server is implemented but was not exercised on
  hardware because the first server always answered.
* The Log screen shows the newest 26 lines; scrolling is not implemented.
* Target hardware (STM32F769I-DISCO) verified: POST 13/13 and "test all" 15/15
  pass, console commands verified over the ST-LINK virtual COM port. Touch
  gestures were not exercised physically.
* Layer "double buffering" is ineffective: HAL_LTDC_LineEventCallback() only
  edits HAL's software copy of the layer configuration (hltdc->LayerCfg) and
  reloads shadow registers, so CFBAR never changes and drawing goes straight to
  the visible front buffer. The line event is also re-armed mid-frame rather
  than in vertical blanking. Enabling real swapping requires drawing into the
  back buffer and programming CFBAR through HAL_LTDC_SetAddress_NoReload();
  left unchanged to preserve current display behavior.
* QSPI boot, HDMI, and remaining board peripherals are outside this change.
